  "spatial_partition_note": "The main comparison builds the spatial partition ONCE (KMeans seeded with --seed0) and reuses it for every model, seed and scheme, so random-vs-spatial is a paired comparison. The SD across seeds of a spatial-CV cell therefore measures learner randomness only and is exactly 0.000 for deterministic models (ridge, PLS). The two uncertainties the fixed partition hides are reported separately: where the block boundaries fall (results.partition_sensitivity, methods kmeans_seed and grid_offset) and how many blocks are used (results.block_sensitivity). Family kmeans_seed: 2 KMeans seeds at 10 blocks (partitions compared up to cluster renumbering, which is arbitrary) gave 1 distinct partition, so the reported k-means blocking is insensitive to its own seed and the 0.000 SD of the main table is a MEASURED NULL (the estimate did not move), not evidence that fold placement cannot matter. Family grid_offset: boundaries redrawn with a random rotation and grid origin at a 7x7 grain, 2 seed run(s) -> 2 distinct partition(s); spread: random forest R2 0.695 +/- 0.030; ridge R2 0.498 +/- 0.040. Both families are in results.partition_sensitivity (per-partition rows in results.partition_rows, learner seed held constant), and the effect of the block COUNT is in results.block_sensitivity.",
